    <string name="usb_general_steps_title">General steps</string>
    <string name="usb_general_steps_content">1. Open Settings\n2. Find About phone or System\n3. Tap Build number 7 times\n4. Enter your lock screen PIN if asked\n5. Go back and open Developer options\n6. Turn on USB debugging</string>
    <string name="usb_brand_specific_title">For your device</string>
    <string name="usb_steps_xiaomi">Xiaomi / Redmi:\n\n1. Settings → My device\n2. Tap All specs\n3. Tap MIUI version 7 times\n4. Go back → Additional settings → Developer options\n5. Enable USB debugging and USB installation\n\n⚠️ Some models require a Xiaomi account and an internet connection</string>
    <string name="usb_steps_huawei">Huawei:\n\n1. Settings → About phone\n2. Tap Build number 7 times\n3. Go back → System &amp; updates → Developer options\n4. Enable USB debugging\n\n⚠️ HarmonyOS: System → Developer options</string>
    <string name="usb_steps_honor">Honor:\n\n1. Settings → About phone\n2. Tap Build number 7 times\n3. Go back → System &amp; updates → Developer options\n4. Enable USB debugging\n\n⚠️ MagicOS paths may differ slightly</string>
    <string name="usb_steps_oppo">OPPO:\n\n1. Settings → About phone\n2. Find Build number or Version info\n3. Tap Build number 7 times\n4. Go back → Additional settings → Developer options\n5. Enable USB debugging\n\n⚠️ ColorOS paths may differ by version</string>
    <string name="usb_steps_vivo">vivo:\n\n1. Settings → System management → About phone\n2. Find Software version\n3. Tap it 7 times\n4. Go back → System management → Developer options\n5. Enable USB debugging\n\n⚠️ Some models also need USB pointer simulation enabled</string>
    <string name="usb_steps_oneplus">OnePlus:\n\n1. Settings → About phone\n2. Tap Build number 7 times\n3. Go back → System → Developer options\n4. Enable USB debugging\n\n⚠️ OxygenOS and ColorOS paths differ slightly</string>
    <string name="usb_steps_samsung">Samsung:\n\n1. Settings → About phone\n2. Open Software information\n3. Tap Build number 7 times\n4. Go back and open Developer options\n5. Enable USB debugging\n\n⚠️ One UI layout may differ by version</string>
    <string name="usb_steps_meizu">Meizu:\n\n1. Settings → About phone\n2. Tap Build number 7 times\n3. Go back → Accessibility → Developer options\n4. Enable USB debugging</string>
    <string name="usb_steps_realme">realme:\n\n1. Settings → About phone\n2. Find Build number\n3. Tap it 7 times\n4. Go back → Additional settings → Developer options\n5. Enable USB debugging</string>
    <string name="usb_tips_title">Tips</string>
    <string name="usb_tips_content">• If you cannot find Build number, search in Settings\n• Some phones need a SIM card and internet to enable this\n• When you connect to a computer, tap Allow on the USB debugging prompt\n• If it still fails, try another USB cable or restart the phone</string>
